    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An unknown author, named Vishnusharma in the prelude, set animal fables inside a frame story to teach three princes nīti, the wise conduct of life.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> version of about 550 CE is lost, but the Arabic Kalīla wa Dimna made from it in about 750 became the source of most European versions.</li><li>Scholars disagree whether it favours unscrupulous Machiavellian politics or demands ethical conduct from those in high office.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A woman leaves her child with a pet mongoose, finds blood on its mouth and kills it, then finds the child alive and the mongoose's blood to be a snake's. The story is told in the fifth book, whose characters are almost all human.</p></aside>
    </section>

