    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 300</span><span class="rf-text">Scholarly consensus places the composition of the Panchatantra around this year, which Patrick Olivelle calls only an educated guess</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 550</span><span class="rf-text">The physician Borzuy translates the work from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a>, the Middle Persian language</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 750</span><span class="rf-text">Ibn al-Muqaffa translates the work into Arabic as Kalīla wa Dimna</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">12th century</span><span class="rf-text">Rabbi Joel makes a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> version, the source of most European versions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1480</span><span class="rf-text">John of Capua's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Directorium Humanae Vitae is printed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1570</span><span class="rf-text">Sir Thomas North publishes the first English translation, The Morall Philosophie of Doni</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">Franklin Edgerton publishes his reconstruction of the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> text</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1997</span><span class="rf-text">Patrick Olivelle's translation, based on Edgerton's reconstruction, is published by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> University Press</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jataka-tales.jpg" alt="Jataka tales" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_India,_Andhra_Pradesh,_possibly_Amaravati,_Satavahana_period_-_Buddha%27s_Previous_Life_as_a_Woodpecker-_Javasakunda_Jataka_-_1970.350_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Panchatantra (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for 'Five Treatises') is an ancient Indian collection of interrelated animal fables in Sanskrit verse and prose, set within a frame story. Its prelude names an octogenarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>, Vishnusharma, as its author, teaching the principles of good government to three princes of Amarasakti, the king of Mahilaropya. Some South Indian recensions and Southeast Asian versions name Vasubhaga instead. Most scholars agree that Vishnusharma is a fictitious name, and Patrick Olivelle says it is unclear whether he was a real person at all. Olivelle and others hold that the author was probably a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, and not a Buddhist or Jain, though probably not a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, since the text neither speaks against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and other deities nor avoids invoking them with reverence.</p><p>Various places of composition have been proposed, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, and the question has been controversial. The text quotes verses identical to some in the Arthasastra, and it draws on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> and Artha shastras and on folk and literary storytelling. Legends with anthropomorphic animals appear in texts from the early centuries of the 1st millennium BCE, such as chapter 4.1 of the Chandogya Upanishad. It shares many stories with the Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a>, and Olivelle writes that it is uncertain whether its author borrowed from the Jatakas or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, or drew on a common treasury of oral and literary tales.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Panchatantra is a series of interwoven fables, many of which give animals human virtues and vices. Apart from a short introduction it has five parts, each with a main frame story that contains embedded stories, often with further stories inside them, and the characters also quote epigrammatic verses to make a point. For the benefit of three ignorant princes, it illustrates nīti, roughly prudent worldly conduct or 'the wise conduct of life'. Book 1, Mitra-bheda, the longest at about 45% of the work, follows the jackal Damanaka, an unemployed minister in a lion's kingdom, and his moralising companion Karataka, who conspire to break up the lion king's friendships. Book 2, Mitra-samprapti, about 22%, follows a crow, a mouse, a turtle and a deer, and stresses the value of friendship and teamwork.</p><p>Book 3, Kakolukiyam, about 26%, is about war and peace: weaker crows, creatures of the day, defeat the stronger owls by wit, since the crow king heeds his counsellor Ciramjivin and the owl king ignores Raktaksa. Books 4 and 5 together make up about 7%. Book 4, Labdhapranasam, gives negative examples of what to avoid, such as yielding to peer pressure or to soothing words with cunning behind them. Book 5, Aparikshitakarakam, warns against hasty action, with lessons such as getting the facts and not building castles in the air; almost all its characters are human.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Panchatantra&#8221; cites where it covers Content, Book 1: Mitra-bheda, Book 2: Mitra-samprāpti, Book 3: Kākolūkīyam:</p><ul><li>Patrick Olivelle, Pañcatantra: The Book of India's Folk Wisdom (Oxford University Press, 2009)</li><li>Arthur W. (transl) Ryder, The Panchatantra (University of Chicago Press, 1925)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The text's date is uncertain. It was translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> around 550 CE, which gives the latest limit of its existence. The earliest limit is unclear. According to Olivelle, the current scholarly consensus places it around 300 CE, though he reminds readers that this is only an educated guess. Johannes Hertel dated it to around 200 BCE, holding that individual fables were already ancient by then. Gillian Adams writes that it may be a product of the Vedic period but that its age cannot be ascertained with confidence, because the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> version has been lost. It is also said to have approximated its current literary form within the 4th to 6th centuries CE, and no Sanskrit text from before 1000 CE has survived.</p><p>Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> carry varying titles, among them Tantrakhyayika, Panchakhyanaka, Panchakhyana and Tantropakhyana. Hertel found several Indian recensions, among them the oldest available Sanskrit one, the Tantrakhyayika of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and the North Western Family text by the Jain monk Purnabhadra of 1199 CE, which blends and rearranges at least three earlier versions. Edgerton studied all the texts that seemed to go back to the lost Sanskrit original and believed he had reconstructed it, a version known as the Southern Family text. The animal names in the Sanskrit are creative, with double meanings that point both to the animal and to a human personality.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Panchatantra&#8221; cites where it covers Author and chronology:</p><ul><li>Patrick Olivelle, Pañcatantra: The Book of India's Folk Wisdom (Oxford University Press, 1999)</li><li>Arthur W. (transl) Ryder, The Panchatantra (University of Chicago Press, 1925)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing.jpg" alt="Gotthold Ephraim Lessing" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gotthold_Ephraim_Lessing.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anna Rosina de Gasc, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the Indian tradition the Panchatantra is a nitishastra, a treatise on political science and human conduct. Early Western scholars read it in different ways. Hertel thought it had a Machiavellian character, and Edgerton wrote that its so-called morals have no bearing on morality, being unmoral and often immoral, since they glorify shrewdness and practical wisdom in the affairs of life, especially of government. Other scholars dismiss this view as one-sided and read the stories as teaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, or proper moral conduct. Olivelle says that the debate, whether the text supports unscrupulous Machiavellian politics or demands ethical conduct from those holding high office, shows the text's rich ambiguity. Konrad Meisig holds that it has been wrongly presented as a textbook for educating princes in the rules of the Arthasastra, and that it is instead a book for the 'Little Man' to develop niti, and a work of social satire.</p><p>Niklas Bengtsson calls it certainly the first work ever written down for children, whose influence on fables, fairy tales and children's literature has therefore been enormous. The French fabulist Jean de La Fontaine acknowledged in his Second Fables that the greatest part was inspired by Pilpay, an Indian sage. Doris <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Lessing</a> noted that until comparatively recently anyone with a literary education in the West knew the Fables of Bidpai as a great Eastern classic.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Panchatantra&#8221; cites where it covers Sanskrit texts, Translations in English:</p><ul><li>Arthur W. (transl) Ryder, The Panchatantra (University of Chicago Press, 1925)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdowsi.jpg" alt="Ferdowsi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imaginary_depiction_of_the_poet_Firdausi._Folio_from_a_Khavarannama_(The_Book_of_the_East)_of_ibn_Husam_al-Din,_1476-1486_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ca. 1476–86 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The work reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in the Sassanid reign of Anoushiravan, when his physician Borzuy translated it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a>, around 550 CE; one dating gives 570 CE. According to the story in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>'s Shah Nama, Borzuy had gone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in search of a herb that revives the dead and was told that the herb is the scientist and the book the way to knowledge. Hans Bakker places his visit at the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> in the 6th century. His Pahlavi version is now lost. It was translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and then, around 750 CE, into Arabic by Ibn al-Muqaffa as Kalila wa Dimna, in which the jackals' names became the name of the whole work. Ibn al-Muqaffa added a chapter on the trial of Dimna, and the political theorist Jennifer London suggests that he used the fables to express risky political views in a metaphorical way.</p><p>Almost all pre-modern European translations come from the Arabic. A Greek version by Simeon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a> followed in 1080, a Spanish one in 1252 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> one by Rabbi Joel in the 12th century. John of Capua put the Hebrew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Directorium Humanae Vitae, printed in 1480, the source of most European versions. Anton Francesco Doni made an Italian version in 1552, from which Sir Thomas North made the first English translation in 1570. Buddhist monks carried the text north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and Persian prose versions of the 12th and 15th centuries led to a Turkish one.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio.jpg" alt="Giovanni Boccaccio" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_del_Castagno_Giovanni_Boccaccio_c_1450.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrea del Castagno, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>It is called certainly the most frequently translated literary product of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. There is a version in nearly every major language of India and, according to Olivelle, 200 versions in more than 50 languages elsewhere; an older estimate by Jacob gave 112 versions in at least 38 languages. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> it is Tantri Kamandaka, with 360 fables; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> it is Nandaka-prakarana and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> Nang Tantrai. Its stories are also the source of several in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">Arabian Nights</a>, and of many Western nursery rhymes and ballads. Folk tale motifs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a>, La Fontaine and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a> have been linked to it.</p><p>Some stories resemble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aesop">Aesop</a>'s Fables: The Ass in the Panther's Skin, The Broken Pot beside The Milkmaid and Her Pail, and The Gold-Giving Snake beside The Man and the Serpent. Max Muller held that animal fables had their principal source in India, but this single-origin hypothesis is now generally discarded in favour of the view that fable motifs arose independently in many cultures, and the relation to Mesopotamian and Greek fables is hotly debated. In modern times Theodor Benfey used it as the basis of comparative literature. Arthur Ryder's translation of 1925 remains popular, and in the 1990s Chandra Rajan's Penguin version and Patrick Olivelle's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> version of 1997 appeared.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Panchatantra&#8221; cites where it covers Early cross-cultural migrations, How Borzuy brought the work from India, Kalila wa Demna: Middle Persian and Arabic versions, Modern era:</p><ul><li>Arthur W. (transl) Ryder, The Panchatantra (University of Chicago Press, 1925)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Panchatantra is an ancient Indian collection of animal fables in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, in five treatises, by an unknown author, probably composed around 300 CE. Carried through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a>, Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and has been called the most frequently translated literary product of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</em></p>

